{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3144","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3144","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3144\",\"parent_revision\":\"13a94b35e4d684329ef278c4b05ab49f3c129e3c2d4eb7264f5b3fdef5aedb37\",\"root_revision\":\"13a94b35e4d684329ef278c4b05ab49f3c129e3c2d4eb7264f5b3fdef5aedb37\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nDelegation prompts for dispatching SAM task execution are composed by hand as free prose. Nothing produces them, so nothing constrains what goes into one.\\n\\nObserved failure, 2026-08-21: a dispatcher hand-wrote a delegation string that named the plan-level skill `dh:implement-feature` and handed it to a task-level agent. The receiving agent followed the instruction, re-entered the plan-management loop it had just been dispatched out of, and recursively spawned further workers, each of which loaded the same plan-level skill. The session cost roughly $100 before it was stopped.\\n\\nThe delegation prompt was the vector. A skill name that belongs to one workflow level travelled into a prompt for another level as an ordinary string, and nothing in the path could tell the difference \u2014 not the dispatcher writing it, not the receiver reading it.\\n\\nReceiver-side prevention has been attempted and does not work here: an agent cannot observe which workflow level dispatched it, so a rule asking it to refuse a level-inappropriate instruction asks it to classify a condition it cannot see. Dispatcher-side prose helps only where the dispatcher is already reading the workflow file that carries the warning, which covers one known site and not the general case.\\n\\nImpact: every hand-composed dispatch is an opportunity to reproduce this, with no mechanism that would catch it. The cost is unbounded \u2014 the failure mode is recursive, so it compounds until a human intervenes.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: the durable close appears to be making the delegation prompt something a tool returns for a ready task, rather than something a dispatcher writes, so no free-prose string carries a skill name across a level boundary. That would be a change to `sam_schema`, and it has not been scoped.\\n\\nAlternative considered and costed but not chosen: renaming `implement-feature` to `delegate-implementation` so the plan-level skill's name announces its level. That touches 543 occurrences across 124 files, `dh:start-task` already occupies the do-implementation half so no second skill is needed, and a rename makes the wrong dispatch less likely to be written without making it impossible.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **hand-composed delegation prompts let a plan-level skill name reach a task-level agent** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 #3060 over-orchestration incident, 2026-08-21\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"92fbfa920706e0517ef77b0cb3f4e1cfd2a83ed934416f5f3138bb0163c75083\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQLLeEw\"}"}